00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:15.720
What I've learned from my
experience is that trust,

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:17.820
especially within healthcare,
is actually built

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:20.220
from the ground up,
not the top down.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:22.950
So when I started, I built
validation cycles directly

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:25.680
with physical therapists
and clinicians,

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.630
and I was iterating
every single day.

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:30.450
What I've realized is that
breaking into an industry isn't

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:31.980
about having the
best credentials

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:33.660
or years of experience.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:35.850
It's actually about earning
the trust of the people

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:37.500
who feel the pain the most.

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:39.420
And so, to my builders
and founders out there,

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:43.110
my advice to you is simple:
build with genuine curiosity

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:46.050
and deep passion, and
build alongside the people

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:48.750
who can help you
shape the future.

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:49.290
That's great.

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:50.010
DONA SARKAR: Perfect.

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:52.320
HANS YANG: Co-design with your
customers to build trust.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:55.480
That's great advice for a
founder in any industry.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:59.700
Next, Vika, Rohan.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:02.850
Vika, so after 50
customer conversations,

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:05.580
one food safety director
stopped you in your tracks

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.560
with a single sentence.

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:10.320
She said, "We don't
need more data.

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:12.510
We need actions."

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:15.240
So how does a founder know when
they finally heard the insight

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:17.910
that inspires a pivot?

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:18.720
ADVIKA VUPPALA: Honestly,

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:21.330
you know because it reframes
everything you have

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:22.440
already heard.

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:24.780
We'd experienced versions
of this shared sentiment

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:26.730
across dozens of interviews,

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:29.430
but it felt like separate
problems to tackle.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:32.310
When the director said
it clearly, it clicked.

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:34.110
That's when we knew it was real.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:36.060
Not when the feedback
is surprising,

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:40.560
but when one sentence suddenly
explains everyone else.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:44.220
That understanding completely
changed our conversations.

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:48.900
Instead of selling visibility,
we now sell confidence.

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:49.650
HANS YANG: I love that.

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:51.390
That pivot from
selling information

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:52.200
to selling an outcome.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:53.340
That's fantastic.

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:55.290
I also understand your mom
is out there, so do you want

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:56.820
to wave to her really quick?

00:13:56.820 --> 00:13:58.180
Hi, Mom.

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:03.930
So, Patrick, you
have a great story.

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:05.160
Well, maybe not great.

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:09.330
You lost £70,000 of your
own money to a problem

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:11.340
that didn't have a solution yet.

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:14.280
Creators are having their
content copied or stolen,

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:16.980
so you decided to build one.

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:20.400
What convinced you to go
from a victim of a problem

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:22.410
to the builder of a solution?

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:23.910
PATRICK BROWN: So,
yeah, unfortunately,

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:27.990
it cost me £70,000 to really
understand the problem.

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:30.300
My designs were
copied and resold,

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:32.280
and then the insight hit me.

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:34.320
Creators and brands
are losing money

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:37.740
to copyright infringement
they can't even see.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:39.450
Detection tools exist,

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:43.050
but AI-modified copies
slip right past them.

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:44.820
So, instead of
waiting for someone

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:47.790
to build a solution,
I built CopyFlag.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:50.310
This allows us to turn
an invisible problem

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.950
into a visible one.

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:55.410
HANS YANG: I love that point,
turning invisible problems

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:56.880
into visible solutions.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:57.630
That's great.

00:14:57.630 --> 00:14:59.160
That's a great way to
describe the vision

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:01.920
that all the best founders have.

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:03.330
PATRICK BROWN: Thank you.

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:05.940
HANS YANG: So each of you
provided such amazing insights

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:08.610
for all the founders out there,
and I really want to thank you

00:15:08.610 --> 00:15:09.930
for sharing that wisdom.

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:12.600
It's so clear why you were
selected as finalists

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:14.880
for this year's competition.

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:15.660
DONA SARKAR: It is so awesome

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:17.280
to see how these
founders have grown

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.640
through their Imagine
Cup journeys and all

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.710
of the incredible support
they've received from Microsoft

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.080
for Startups and all of you,
their broader community.

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:27.510
Thank you to all of the
mentors and coaches out there.

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:29.220
We appreciate you so much.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:30.210
I think it's time to move

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:32.820
on to the final stage
of the competition.

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:34.020
You all ready?

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:36.180
Yeah. Hans, you ready
to announce the winner?

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:37.020
I didn't look.

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:38.400
HANS YANG: I didn't look.

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:39.930
Are you all ready?

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:40.410
All right.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:42.240
Let's do it.

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:44.730
Okay. Let's see.

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:49.050
These three startups have all
